 ]

 [; ── P9. coming from Python: index-at (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-index-at-wrong level=core
  [title [#Indexing with `@` — `[$array@$i]`#]]
  [tags array index nth one-based python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
`@` is the attribute step of a path (`$row@id`), not a subscript, and a
call head `[$f …]` carries no path step. There is no `xs[i]`. Positional
access is the call `[$nth $xs $i]`, and positions are 1-BASED, as in every
CX position (strings, `re` matches, `[$range]`): `[$nth $xs 1]` is the first
item, and `[$nth $xs 0]` is refused out of range.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $xs [10, 20, 30]] [= $i 2] [$xs@$i]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
error: cx-err:CXER0100: parse: call head `[$fn …]` may not carry path steps; bind the value first at line 1:38
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-index-at-right level=core
  [title [#`[$nth $xs $i]`, counting from 1#]]
  [tags array index nth one-based python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $xs [10, 20, 30]] [= $i 2] [r first=[$nth $xs 1] at-i=[$nth $xs $i]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[r first=10 at-i=20]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P10. coming from Python: fn-params (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-fn-params-wrong level=core
  [title [#`[?fn $a $b …]` — parameters without parentheses#]]
  [tags fn lambda parameters python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
A function's parameters are ONE parenthesised list, then exactly one
body: `[?fn ($a $b) body]`, as in `[?def name ($a $b) body]`. Written bare,
`$b` is read as the body and the real body as a second one, and the form is
refused.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $add [?fn $a $b [+ $a $b]]] [$add 2 3]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0100 message='[?fn]/[?def] takes exactly one body: the form is [?fn (params) body] (code.md §8.6), and a third positional is a second body — a `:body` here is the ATOM :body in body position, not a slot (1558-b, #1558)' at='1:1']
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-fn-params-right level=core
  [title [#Parameters in one list — `[?fn ($a $b) …]`#]]
  [tags fn lambda parameters python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $add [?fn ($a $b) [+ $a $b]]] [$add 2 3]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
5
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P11. coming from Python: call-sigil (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-call-sigil-wrong level=core
  [title [#A call without `$` — `[double 21]`#]]
  [tags call sigil construction python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
A call is `[$f args…]`. Without the `$`, `[double 21]` is not a call of the
function you just defined — it is an ELEMENT named `double`, legal data, so
the program succeeds and answers the element. Every call of a function,
builtin or module verb carries `$`; only operator heads (`+`, `=`, `and`)
and directives (`?`) are written without it.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def double ($c) [* $c 2]]
[double 21]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[double 21]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-call-sigil-right level=core
  [title [#`[$double 21]` — the `$` makes it a call#]]
  [tags call sigil construction python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def double ($c) [* $c 2]]
[$double 21]
#]]
  [out-text [#
42
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P12. coming from Python: for-string (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-for-string-wrong level=core
  [title [#A `[?for]` over a string#]]
  [tags for string characters split python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
A string is one scalar, not a sequence of characters. A generator over it
walks one item — the whole string — so the loop runs once. Split it first:
`[$strings:split $s '']` answers its code points (and `strings:graphemes`
its user-perceived characters).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings' as=strings]
[?for [in $ch 'abc'] [yield [$strings:upper $ch]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
ABC
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-for-string-right level=core
  [title [#Over `[$strings:split $s '']`#]]
  [tags for string characters split python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings' as=strings]
[?for [in $ch [$strings:split 'abc' '']] [yield [$strings:upper $ch]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
A
B
C
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P13. coming from Python: range (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-range-wrong level=core
  [title [#`(1, $n)` as a range#]]
  [tags range sequence for python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
`(1, $n)` is a two-item SEQUENCE — the items `1` and `$n` — not a range, so
the loop runs twice. A range is the call `[$range lo hi]`, and both ends are
INCLUSIVE (`[$range 1 4]` is 1, 2, 3, 4; Python's `range(1, n)` stops before
`n`).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $n 4] [?for [in $i (1, $n)] [yield [* $i $i]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
1
16
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-range-right level=core
  [title [#`[$range 1 $n]`, both ends included#]]
  [tags range sequence for python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $n 4] [?for [in $i [$range 1 $n]] [yield [* $i $i]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
1
4
9
16
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P14. coming from Python: replace-regex (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-replace-regex-wrong level=core
  [title [#`strings:replace` with a regular expression#]]
  [tags strings replace regex re python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
`strings:replace` replaces a LITERAL substring. Handed a pattern it looks
for the characters `[0-9]+` themselves, finds none, and answers the string
unchanged — no error. Pattern replacement is `re:replace` over a compiled
pattern from `[$re:compile]`.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings' as=strings]
[$strings:replace 'a1b22c333' '[0-9]+' '#']
#]]
  [out-text [#
a1b22c333
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-replace-regex-right level=core
  [title [#`re:replace` with `[$re:compile …]`#]]
  [tags strings replace regex re python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/re' as=re]
[$re:replace [$re:compile '[0-9]+'] 'a1b22c333' '#']
#]]
  [out-text [#
'a#b#c#'
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P15. coming from Python: find-all-string (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-find-all-string-wrong level=core
  [title [#`re:find-all` with a pattern string#]]
  [tags re find-all compile python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
Every `re` verb that matches takes a COMPILED pattern, never a pattern
string: compile once with `[$re:compile]` and pass the value (bind it with
`[?const]` to reuse it). A string there is refused, naming the verb and
`re:compile`. `re:find-all` answers `[match …]` elements; `re:find-all-text`
answers just the matched texts, Python's `re.findall`.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/re' as=re]
[$re:find-all '[0-9]+' 'a1b22c333']
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0100 message='re:find-all: argument 1 expects a compiled regex from [$re:compile] (compile the pattern string first), got string — operand-kind fault (E_ARG)' at='2:1']
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [case id=ap-py-find-all-string-right level=core
  [title [#Compile the pattern first#]]
  [tags re find-all compile python gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/re' as=re]
[?let [= $digits [$re:compile '[0-9]+']]
  [?for [in $m [$re:find-all $digits 'a1b22c333']] [yield [r start=$m@start end=$m@end]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[r start=2 end=2]
[r start=4 end=5]
[r start=7 end=9]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── P16. coming from Python: if-true-false (issue 1813 §27) ── ]

 [case id=ap-py-if-true-false-wrong level=core
  [title [#`[?if C [then true] [else false]]`#]]
  [tags if boolean redundant python antipattern gaps-1 issue-1813]
  [why [#
A comparison already answers a boolean. Wrapping it in an `[?if]` that maps
`true` to `true` and `false` to `false` adds a branch and changes nothing —
both halves below answer the same value. Write the condition itself.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
